    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a42fc42f-664e-587c-a0b1-fcc8968baead",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- x - y + 1?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-0-policy-2-4c6625aaf57ad834/runtime/package/17592186172040/plots/b450dd91ae973d1b29f184458d0f3634f7e2f260f853c11d4d7c28c470af6344.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-0-policy-2-4c6625aaf57ad834/runtime/package/17592186172040/plots/1649b5fc045853645f515f8f6ac8a2a753fc43e965a800c86dd87acb28232dbe.png)",
